Z||000000||deceler8||09-07-2000||09:26 PM||||This just happened yesterday (actually just prior to Ogre's "chime" article)...<P>Used the accessory position to turn on the radio (to check speakers)...turned key to off and attempted to remove it...key would barely turn to the off position and I really had to yank it out...during this, key would not turn, nor would the cylinder.<P>So I did get the key out, but the cylinder was still frozen. So I sprayed some WD40 into the cylinder, inserted the key, and it worked as if nothing had happened. But now my "chime" won't turn off...<P>Is my switch going bad ? And if so, is it easy to replace...25 words or less easy ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||DjKid||09-07-2000||09:49 PM||||Yes, hehe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A>||||reg||10
Z||000002||theogre||09-07-2000||11:52 PM||||The process of getting the column apart is in the Haynes book. Also here-&gt;&gt; <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>Oliver's Fiero Pages</A><P>I have additional notes in my cave.<P>sounds like either the keylock broke, or the gears on the end of the lock broke. The pin in the lock that hits the chime could have let go. <P>Posibly the ignition switch let go. The switch isn't that likely. <P>Expect problems getting it apart especially if the key sensor pin fell out of the lock.||||reg||5
